In C, a program may be terminated in several standard ways, depending on context:

```c
return 0;
```

from `main` is the conventional successful termination:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    printf("Finished.\n");
    return 0;
}
```

You may also call `exit()` from `<stdlib.h>`:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(void) {
    exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);
}
```

Useful status values include:

```c
exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);  // normal success
exit(EXIT_FAILURE);  // failure
```

For immediate termination without normal cleanup handlers or buffered-output flushing, use `_Exit()`:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

_Exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
```

This was analogous to a commander ordering an abrupt withdrawal: unlike `exit()`, `_Exit()` did not execute functions registered with `atexit()` and did not flush standard I/O streams.

For most ordinary programs, prefer:

```c
return 0;
```

from `main`, or `return 1;` / `EXIT_FAILURE` when reporting an error.
